+# 0002 — A failed attempt waits, and a broken endpoint is cut off
+
+- **Status:** accepted (step `relay/2.8-retry-budget`)
+- **Context:** the policy this decides lives in [src/backoff.rs](../../src/backoff.rs), the worker
+  that spends it in [src/delivery.rs](../../src/delivery.rs) and [src/claim.rs](../../src/claim.rs),
+  and the states it writes down are [migrations/0005_deliveries.sql](../../migrations/0005_deliveries.sql).
+  It is the other half of [0001 — a delivery is at least once](0001-at-least-once.md): that record
+  decided deliveries are retried, this one decides how.
+
+## Decision
+
+A failed attempt is retried under a **budget**, and the budget has three parts.
+
+**A wait that grows, spread across the fleet.** After the first failure a delivery waits somewhere
+in the upper half of `base` (30 seconds by default) and that window doubles with every further
+failure, up to a cap of six hours. Where in the window a delivery lands is a *jitter* derived from a
+hash of `(delivery id, attempt number)`: it is the same number on every worker, in every process,
+and on every rerun of a test, and it is different for deliveries that have nothing to do with each
+other. It is decorrelation, not secrecy.
+
+The window is `[base/2, base]` rather than the textbook `[0, base]` on purpose. Full jitter
+occasionally answers a wait of almost nothing, and a wait of nothing is a spin: the retry lands in
+the same millisecond as the failure it is retrying, the subscriber that just hiccuped gets the same
+request again, and — because the spec of `relay/2.6-at-least-once` asks a worker for a second turn
+*immediately* after a failure and expects the delivery to be waiting — the course's own earlier step
+turns red. Half of the window is a floor; the upper half is where the herd is broken up.
+
+**A bounded number of attempts.** A delivery is given at most `max_attempts` failures (5 by
+default, the constant `relay/2.6-at-least-once` wrote down), after which it is buried with the
+reason and the count on the row. This is what makes the guarantee "at least once, until given up
+on" instead of "forever".
+
+**A breaker per endpoint.** Consecutive *retryable* failures at one endpoint open a breaker for a
+cooldown (5 failures, 30 seconds by default). While it is open, attempts to that endpoint are
+**shed**: the delivery is not attempted, and — this is the whole point — it is not charged for the
+attempt it did not get. Its `attempts` does not move and it goes straight back to the queue. When
+the cooldown has passed the breaker is half open and lets exactly **one** probe through; a probe
+that is taken closes the breaker and forgets the failures, a probe that fails opens it again for a
+fresh cooldown, and a probe that is never reported (the worker died holding it) is presumed lost
+after another cooldown.
+
+The breaker counts failures a retry would have hit again, and nothing else: a `410 Gone` or a `404`
+is a statement about one delivery, not about the endpoint's health, and the deliveries behind a
+`410` still deserve their attempts.
+
+## Alternatives
+
+**No jitter, one wait per attempt number.** The simplest backoff, and the one that fails worst: a
+subscriber that restarts comes back to a fleet that has been retrying on a fixed schedule and is
+hit by every delivery at once, in the same second. The wait is also exactly predictable, so a
+subscriber that is down for an hour knows precisely when the next wave arrives. Rejected: the herd
+is the thing being avoided.
+
+**Random jitter from an `Rng` (full jitter, `[0, base]`).** The textbook recommendation, and it
+decorrelates as well as the hash does. Two costs, and both are real: a retry that fires immediately
+(the floor above), and the loss of reproducibility — a test, a log line and an operator's
+reconstruction of an incident all need to know *when* the next attempt is, and a number drawn from
+a generator inside the worker is not knowable to anybody else. Rejected in favour of the hash.
+
+**Decorrelated jitter** (`[base, 3 × previous delay]`). Spreads as well as anything here and needs
+per-delivery state to remember the previous delay. The state this service already keeps per
+delivery is `attempts`, so a delay that is a pure function of `(delivery, attempts)` needs no new
+column and cannot be corrupted by a partial write. Rejected for the state, not for the spread.
+
+**No breaker: retry each delivery until its own budget runs out.** What `relay/2.6-at-least-once`
+left in place, and honest for a queue that is mostly healthy. Against an endpoint that is gone,
+every delivery in the queue spends its whole budget — sixteen claimable rows cost eighty requests,
+and the queue drains at the speed of the failure rather than the speed of the successes. The
+breaker is what turns "the subscriber is down" into "the subscriber is down and the queue is
+waiting for it".
+
+**A breaker that opens on any failure, or on a rate of failures.** Opening on the first failure
+makes a single hiccup an outage; a rate over a window needs the window's state, its buckets and its
+clock, and it answers "is this endpoint broken" with a number an operator cannot reproduce by hand.
+Consecutive failures are the version a person can check in one query, and a success resets them.
+
+**Cutting off the endpoint in the claim** (skip a broken endpoint's rows in the `SELECT … FOR UPDATE
+SKIP LOCKED`) instead of releasing them afterwards. Fewer round trips, and the claim's signature —
+pinned by `relay/2.5-atomic-claim`, whose spec calls it — has no room for a set of endpoints to
+avoid. The release is the smaller change and it keeps the claim a claim about work, not about
+health.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- **The budget's numbers are one worker's opinions, and they are shared.** Everything that runs a
+  turn takes one `Budget`; the breaker's memory is in the clone, so a worker that builds a fresh
+  budget per turn has a breaker that resets per turn and protects nothing. `tick` — the one-shot
+  entry `relay/2.6-at-least-once` pinned — is the default policy with no memory: it is right for a
+  test and wrong for a loop.
+- **A shed delivery is invisible unless it is counted.** Nothing moved on the row, so the only
+  trace that the worker looked at it and decided not to try is `Tick::shed`. A deployment that
+  does not export that number reads an open breaker as "no work".
+- **The queue looks longer than it is.** Shed deliveries sit in `pending` next to deliveries that
+  have never been attempted, and the difference between "waiting for its turn" and "waiting for the
+  endpoint to come back" is only in the breaker. The metric that separates them belongs to
+  `relay/2.18-delivery-metrics`.
+- **The cooldown is the latency floor for a recovery.** An endpoint that comes back right after its
+  third failure waits out the rest of the cooldown before the probe finds it — thirty seconds by
+  default, and a longer one if the breaker keeps re-opening. That is the price of not hammering a
+  subscriber that is still starting.
+- **Half of every window is dead time.** Waiting at least `base/2` means a subscriber that was
+  briefly away still waits fifteen seconds before the first retry. The alternative is the spin
+  above, and a service that retries instantly is a service that cannot tell "not yet" from "no".
+- **Shedding is not free.** The turn still claimed the row and still wrote it back, so an open
+  breaker costs one claim and one update per delivery per turn. That is the cheap version of a
+  broken endpoint, not a free one.
+
+## What would make us revisit this
+
+A subscriber population where the recovery latency matters more than the load — a payment
+confirmation, say — would want a longer window and a shorter cooldown, per endpoint rather than one
+policy for the fleet. And if the queue ever runs as several processes, the breaker becomes a
+per-process opinion and needs either partitioning or shared state; the moment one endpoint is
+served by two processes, "consecutive failures" stops being a fact about the endpoint and starts
+being a fact about a process's half of it.
